1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
And now it's the old scout master.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
So here we are at our campsite

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
And this guy comes riding through on a horse

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,000
And he stops and talks with us a little while

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:19,000
And, yeah, it's a beautiful horse,

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:24,000
But the horse does. you know what horses do?

7
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,000
The guy's getting ready to ride away.

8
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,000
I look at what the horse left there on the ground

9
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,000
And I say to the rider I said, hey, buddy,

10
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,000
are you going to leave that lion here?

11
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:38,000
And he looks at me and he says no, it's a horse

12
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,000
And I'm taking it with me.

13
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,000
I'm sorry.

14
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Hey, this is podcast number 123..

15
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Welcome back to the Scout Master podcast.

16
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
This is Clark Green.

17
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Hey, let's take a look at the mailbag here.

18
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Well, we got a few messages about our last podcast,

19
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
which was a scout master panel discussion about parent issues,

20
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
And Peter LaRue wrote in and he said:

21
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,000
great show about dealing with parents.

22
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
It can be particularly tough for younger scouts.

23
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
There's some nice pointers there.

24
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
One tip I found helpful with difficult parents

25
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,000
is to ask them to get involved running things

26
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:49,000
that they're unhappy about.

27
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:51,000
This has one of two results:

28
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,000
They either backpedal and leave you alone

29
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,000
or get involved and often surprise you with their involvement.

30
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:57,000
True,

31
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:58,000
True,

32
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,000
And Greg Sepley backs you up, Peter,

33
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,000
because Greg wrote in and said:

34
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Peter has a great point.

35
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:05,000
What I usually do after they complain

36
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,000
or get vocal about something

37
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,000
is ask them what they would do in my place

38
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:13,000
And then, after they tell me what they would do,

39
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
I say: you're hired,

40
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,000
That's great.

41
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,000
This almost always is followed by them

42
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,000
taking the whole thing down a notch.

43
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Thanks, Greg,

44
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Nice little word of wisdom there.

45
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Ann Olson wrote in again and said:

46
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
this show came in handy for us this week.

47
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,000
That's great to hear.

48
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,000
And we also heard from Derek Morton

49
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
who said really great stuff.

50
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,000
I'm amazed every week how relevant your podcasts are.

51
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Thank you

52
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
Well, thank you for being in touch, Derek.

53
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:50,000
And Steve Mahoney wrote in.

54
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
He said: hey, Clark, I'm currently a Cub Scouter,

55
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,000
but I listened to your podcast

56
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,000
to understand the Scouting program better.

57
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,000
You're a big help.

58
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Thanks very much.

59
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,000
Thanks very much, Steve.

60
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
I really appreciate you listening in.

61
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
So in this podcast

62
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,000
we're going back to podcast number 74..

63
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Remember podcast number 74, how we all loved it.

64
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,000
Yes, I remember it well.

65
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
And we're going back to hear

66
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
an episode of Scout MasterChip

67
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,000
in seven minutes or less dealing with summer camp,

68
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Because you know it's that time of year

69
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
started thinking about summer camp.

70
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
I got a great summer camp story to tell you,

71
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,000
And I have a couple of reflections

72
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:35,000
gleaned from an email question

73
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,000
that I received this week.

74
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:38,000
So that's enough for this podcast,

75
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Wouldn't you say,

76
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Because that's what I'm saying,

77
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,000
That's what I got for you.

78
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,000
So let's get started, shall we?

79
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Scout MasterChip in seven minutes

80
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,000
or less.

81
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
One of the most fun things I've gotten to do

82
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
in scouting was be on our camp staff,

83
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,000
And I was able to do that for about 12 years

84
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,000
as a director in different departments

85
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,000
and as a camp director,

86
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,000
And so the advice I'm about to give you

87
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,000
is kind of based on watching hundreds of different troops

88
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,000
and thousands of different scout leaders

89
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
over those 12 years come and experience

90
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,000
a week of summer camp.

91
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,000
There's some common difficulties and problems

92
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,000
that arise,

93
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
And there's some things that you can do

94
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
that will just make things go a lot better for you

95
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
while you're there at camp with your scouts,

96
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,000
And scout camp is a great place to go

97
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:35,000
if you're a scout.

98
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:36,000
It's fun,

99
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:37,000
There's lots of challenges,

100
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:38,000
There's great stuff to do,

101
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,000
And it's the same as when you're an adult.

102
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
It's just a little bit of a different level.

103
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Now, if it's the first couple of times

104
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:47,000
you go to scout camp, it can be really stressful.

105
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:49,000
It can be. you know. you can be faced

106
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,000
with a lot of new things, a lot of things

107
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:52,000
that you're unfamiliar with.

108
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,000
You're not quite sure what the expectations are.

109
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,000
So you do have to take real good care of yourself.

110
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Now that's if you've been going to camp,

111
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,000
you know if this year is your first time

112
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:03,000
or your 25th year going.

113
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
You have to take good care of yourself physically,

114
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
because if you take good care of yourself physically

115
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
you're going to be in good shape mentally

116
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,000
to handle camp.

117
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Our camp is one of the only remaining ones

118
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,000
that has a seven day schedule.

119
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
We bring everybody in on Sunday

120
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
and they leave on the following Sunday,

121
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:26,000
So somebody gets to camp.

122
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,000
You know your average adult leader gets to camp

123
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,000
on a Sunday afternoon

124
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
And yeah, first day of camp.

125
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:33,000
that's kind of stressful.

126
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:34,000
There's a lot of things to do,

127
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:35,000
There's new things to learn,

128
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:39,000
There's people to meet, places to get to,

129
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,000
And you might feel a little bit rushed

130
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,000
and by the time you're headed off to bed that night

131
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
you're probably pretty tired

132
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
and you may sleep well and you may not.

133
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:50,000
And you know the food's going to be a little different,

134
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:52,000
The water's going to be a little different.

135
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Your latrine's going to be a little different.

136
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
You're going to have a lot of the same experiences

137
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
that the boys do with unfamiliar stressors

138
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
and things like that.

139
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,000
But you're also going to be older than they are.

140
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
I don't know if you notice that.

141
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,000
So you know your abilities are going to be compromised

142
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
maybe by being a bit older than they are,

143
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
being a little more set in your ways,

144
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
and you could become cranky real fast.

145
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
And how do I know this?

146
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
Do I have to tell you?

147
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,000
I mean, I've been there,

148
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:24,000
I've done it,

149
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,000
I've not taken very good care of myself physically

150
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
and then I get real cranky

151
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
and yeah, then things just get ugly.

152
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:37,000
So if you are hot, tired, hungry, thirsty,

153
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,000
you need to get cool, you need to get rested,

154
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:42,000
you need to get fed and you need to get hydrated.

155
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
And that's going to be a pretty big service

156
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
to you yourself personally,

157
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
your fellow leaders, your troop and the camp staff.

158
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,000
If you're not cranky and mean,

159
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:56,000
it's going to make the whole week go a lot easier.

160
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:00,000
So that's one key piece of advice.

161
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,000
You're coming into a situation

162
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:04,000
that you may not be familiar with,

163
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
but you've got a staff there,

164
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
people who are adults

165
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
and who've been at it for a long time.

166
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,000
and then you've got young adults,

167
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,000
guys who are around college age,

168
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,000
who've got a lot of energy

169
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,000
and may have been at the camp staff job

170
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:22,000
for several years.

171
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:25,000
and then you've got a bunch of younger staff members

172
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,000
who are basically learning right along with your scouts.

173
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,000
They're learning a lot of things

174
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:30,000
right along with your scouts.

175
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,000
Now they probably are a little bit farther ahead of them

176
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,000
as far as the camp things go,

177
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,000
but maturity-wise and things like that,

178
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:38,000
they're going to be more like

179
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:41,000
your average 15,, 16,, 17-year-old

180
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,000
And you've got to take that into account.

181
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:46,000
You need to be considerate and supportive of them

182
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,000
in the same way that you're considerate

183
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:49,000
and supportive of your scouts.

184
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:51,000
You never, ever want to step in

185
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
and be raid or criticize or argue

186
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
with a camp staff member.

187
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
You don't want to do that with a youth leader

188
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:58,000
in your troop.

189
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:00,000
You don't want to do that with a fellow adult leader

190
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
and you certainly don't want to do that

191
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:03,000
in the presence of scouts.

192
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,000
And now model how much you may think

193
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:09,000
that that staff member deserves it.

194
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:11,000
What you want to do is you want to have

195
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:14,000
either a quiet talk with them or their boss,

196
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:18,000
at an appropriate distance from the scouts

197
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,000
and that will usually solve the problem,

198
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:23,000
And sometimes that might be your problem.

199
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
You have to realize that camp staff members

200
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:28,000
are totally dedicated to making your stay worthwhile

201
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:30,000
and your idea of what might be right

202
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:32,000
and their idea of what might be right

203
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:33,000
could be two different things.

204
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:36,000
So you kind of got to go with the flow a little bit.

205
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,000
You got to join in the program

206
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
and encourage your scouts to do so,

207
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,000
even if the program, in your humble opinion,

208
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,000
isn't all that great.

209
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,000
If you go to a scout camp

210
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:47,000
and you eat in a dining hall,

211
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
there's usually a program around the dining hall time

212
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:52,000
and you know it may be

213
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,000
a really great whizbang, high energy program

214
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
and it might not be all that great.

215
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
but what really encourages a lackluster

216
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,000
kind of difficult program is a lively audience.

217
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:06,000
A lively audience goes a long, long way

218
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,000
and you and your troop, with the right attitude,

219
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
could be the catalyst that makes for a great week

220
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
for everybody in camp.

221
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:16,000
You know, in the vast scheme of things

222
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:19,000
I know that as a staff member

223
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
and as a directory camp

224
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,000
we always emphasize that we were serving scouts

225
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,000
and scout leaders

226
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,000
and we were doing our dangest

227
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,000
to make them comfortable

228
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
and to make things work well for them.

229
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,000
Now, that's being said,

230
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,000
You don't want to go pulling rank

231
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:38,000
as an adult or a scout leader.

232
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:40,000
You don't want to ask for special privileges.

233
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,000
You don't want to try and impress the staff members

234
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
with your self-importance.

235
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
A lot of people instantly respect

236
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
your position as a scout leader

237
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,000
and they're going to try

238
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
and do their best for you.

239
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
Just don't make a big giant deal out of it.

240
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
There's really no rank to be pulled there

241
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
and remember that these guys are working hard

242
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
trying to help you,

243
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
So just go easy.

244
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:07,000
You need to look for the opportunities

245
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:10,000
to participate with a lot of gusto

246
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,000
and a lot of spirit in ceremonies

247
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,000
and traditions and practices of the camp,

248
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,000
even if they're unfamiliar to you.

249
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:19,000
If it's the first time you end up at a scout camp,

250
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
and maybe it's a new one that you're trying out

251
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
with your troop, get into it,

252
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
Jump right in.

253
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
Camps and camp staff members

254
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
are very proud of their traditions

255
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
and you don't want to try to impose

256
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
the way that everybody did it

257
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
at the last camp you were at

258
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
or the way that you think it should be done.

259
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,000
Let them call the shots,

260
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
Let them run the show and just jump in

261
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
and have a great time with it.

262
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
You'll be a camp with a lot of different scout troops

263
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
who will be run one way,

264
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
but you might be doing things a different way

265
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,000
and sometimes little inner troop rivalries

266
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
may develop.

267
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
Now, scout age boys, as you know,

268
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,000
they're pretty hyper competitive

269
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
and they need to be reminded about

270
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,000
fairness and courtesy and sportsmanship

271
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
every once in a while.

272
