sign many handbooks.

561
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
the second problem: I see,

562
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
and I think I understand

563
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
why it's happened,

564
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
but I have to wonder why this parent is so involved

565
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
in this.

566
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
I mean, why aren't they, instead of their son,

567
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
dealing with the scout master

568
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
and making these arrangements?

569
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
again, we're in Weeblows territory,

570
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
not Boy Scout territory.

571
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
I would-

572
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
and I say this as kindly and

573
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
understandingly as possible- encourage

574
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
them to allow

575
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
the boy, allow that

576
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
scout, the autonomy to make these decisions

577
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
and plans for himself.

578
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
he'll certainly learn and grow

579
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
a great deal more if he does.

580
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
and number three:

581
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
what's the source of the conflict here?

582
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
what's the problem?

583
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
well, because this particular scout master

584
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
has reserved all authority to himself

585
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
and is not-

586
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
and this is my opinion, based only on

587
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
what this parent has told me-

588
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
the scout program functioned properly

589
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
and that causes

590
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
parents to become defensive

591
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
and over involved,

592
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
and they get over

593
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
involved in charting their son's ways forward

594
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
and then inevitably

595
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
this is going to turn into a conflict somewhere.

596
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
now, if you listen to the last

597
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
podcast and the scout master panel

598
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
discussion we had about parent issues,

599
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
if you're following the program,

600
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
the program stands on its own and defends itself.

601
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
if you're not following

602
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
the program- like as I said

603
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
in my judgment based on this email,

604
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
this particular scout master

605
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
not following the program-

606
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
then you're opening yourself

607
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
up to these kind of arguments and difficulties.

608
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
some parents

609
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
are going to be over involved

610
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
and you have to kind of explain the

611
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
program to them and ask them to step back

612
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
a little bit and see if you can get

613
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
them to work with that, and in

614
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
99% of the instances you're

615
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
going to. so my final

616
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
advice to this parent is:

617
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,000
stop arguing.

618
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
don't stop arguing

619
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,000
with the scout master. stop bothering the

620
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,000
committee chair about this. this is

621
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
snowballed into this kind of conflict

622
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
because nobody's right,

623
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
because

624
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
they're not following

625
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
the scout program.

626
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
okay, I. why

627
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
are they doing requirements

628
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
at a troop meeting? now everybody's

629
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
going to say, Clark, everybody does

630
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
requirements at a troop meeting. well,

631
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
yeah, kind of maybe, but

632
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
we're not in business

633
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,000
to do requirements.

634
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
we do the things that scouts do

635
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
and that just happens to be requirements.

636
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
parent mentioned in the email

637
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
an official orienteering course

638
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,000
with the compass and everything like that.

639
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:24,000
there's no such thing.

640
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
there's no such thing.

641
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
there is an orienteering course that the

642
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,000
requirement describes,

643
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,000
and you just do that in the course of a

644
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,000
thing, or maybe you do it at a troop meeting

645
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
or something like that. but why are you doing it?

646
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,000
not, though, so that scouts can

647
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
fulfill requirements, but so that scouts

648
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
can do the things that scouts

649
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:44,000
do.

650
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
that's how they advance in scouting:

651
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,000
not by fulfilling requirements,

652
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
but by doing the things that

653
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
scouts do. now, if you

654
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:54,000
find yourself toe to toe

655
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,000
with the way that a troop is operating,

656
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:58,000
you have two basic options:

657
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,000
you stay with the current troop

658
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,000
and accept that you don't like the way that

659
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
they do things, or you find another troop

660
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,000
that better suits your idea of scouting.

661
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:08,000
to me, that decision

662
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:10,000
is always up to the scout.

663
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:12,000
now, recently,

664
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
I had a boy in my troop

665
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:16,000
who was getting discouraged with scouting

666
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,000
and really didn't enjoy it anymore because

667
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
the friends that had crossed over

668
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
with him from weevilos

669
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
and who had joined their troop

670
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
had left.

671
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
they decided that they weren't interested

672
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:30,000
in scouting anymore and they quit scouts,

673
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
and he was the only one left,

674
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,000
and his father was quite involved in everything like that

675
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,000
and dad came to us

676
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
and he said: you know, this is a real problem

677
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,000
for me because I want to continue on.

678
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:42,000
I was an eagle scout. I'd love my son to be an eagle scout,

679
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:44,000
but he's just not interested in this anymore

680
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
because none of his friends are there.

681
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
and that's the only reason

682
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,000
that a boy- you know, when a boy is 11

683
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,000
or 12 or years old,

684
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
he gets the idea that

685
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,000
scouting is fun,

686
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
perhaps that he's building his character

687
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
and stuff like that.

688
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,000
but what's his motive for being involved?

689
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
his friends are involved.

690
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
he had a buddy in school who was involved with this other troop

691
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,000
and so I told dad

692
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,000
I said: well, what about the other troop?

693
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,000
does he have any friends that are involved there?

694
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
and they went and visited that find out that he has friends.

695
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:16,000
so they moved to the other troop.

696
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,000
no problem, no problem,

697
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,000
the boys still in scouts.

698
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:22,000
dad will be able to be involved with him,

699
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:24,000
even though dad would rather

700
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:26,000
stay with our troop because

701
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,000
dad likes the way that we do things.

702
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
so if you're going to make a decision about this,

703
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,000
it starts with the scout.

704
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,000
listen, you're in the troop with your friends.

705
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:36,000
I'm having a little problem

706
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,000
seeing that my way forward.

707
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
I don't think that they're doing things quite the way

708
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,000
that I envision them.

709
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:44,000
do you want to stay there with your friends,

710
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:46,000
or shall we look for another troop?

711
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:48,000
maybe the answer is going to come back

712
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:50,000
and you don't like the answer, which is

713
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,000
no, I want to stay with my friends.

714
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,000
even in a troop that's not

715
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:56,000
strictly applying the scouting program,

716
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,000
boys get a good experience.

717
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:00,000
boys are still going to have a fine

718
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:02,000
experience out of it.

719
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,000
we're talking about, sometimes,

720
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:06,000
degrees of things here, aren't we?

721
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:08,000
so let's just go back over this again.

722
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:10,000
there are three problems here.

723
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:12,000
obviously, this

724
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,000
troop, in my opinion,

725
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:16,000
based on the email that I read,

726
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:18,000
is not following the scout program

727
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:20,000
in the optimal way that it should happen.

728
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:22,000
this causes conflict

729
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:24,000
and

730
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:26,000
the conflict is kind of irresolvable

731
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:28,000
because they're not following the program.

732
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:30,000
and because they're not following the program,

733
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:32,000
a parent is getting over

734
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:34,000
involved

735
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:36,000
and that causes conflict too

736
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:38,000
and it's kind of irresolvable

737
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:40,000
why we're not following the program.

738
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:42,000
so the options

739
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:44,000
basically come down to this:

740
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:46,000
you stick with this troop

741
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:48,000
where, if you just can't see your way through,

742
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,000
you find something that better suits

743
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:52,000
your idea of scouting.

744
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:54,000
you're not going to change this troop

745
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:56,000
and, forgive me,

746
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:58,000
I've probably said this

747
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:00,000
any number of times on the blog and the podcast,

748
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:02,000
but I'm going to repeat it again:

749
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:04,000
for a parent

750
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:06,000
to get in

751
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:08,000
and to try and change

752
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:10,000
the way that a troop operates-

753
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:12,000
especially if it's a troop of long standing

754
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:14,000
and they have a certain way of operating-

755
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:16,000
is pretty much a fool's errand.

756
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:18,000
most of the time

757
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:20,000
some people make it work,

758
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:22,000
but it's because they are very patient

759
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:24,000
and they work very carefully

760
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:26,000
and they go forward

761
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:28,000
very incrementally.

762
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:30,000
the people with authority

763
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:32,000
within the organizational structure of scouting

764
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:34,000
are the chartered organization represented,

765
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:36,000
the troop committee chairman

766
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:38,000
and the head of the chartered organization.

767
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:40,000
if they're not interested in making changes,

768
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:42,000
no changes are going to be made,

769
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:44,000
and so you're just going to be creating

770
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:46,000
a lot of heat and very little light.

771
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:48,000
so

772
00:28:48,000 --> 00:28:50,000
my advice to people is usually:

773
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:52,000
look, you don't really jibe

774
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:54,000
with the way that they're doing things.

775
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:56,000
find another place where it works better for you.

776
00:28:56,000 --> 00:28:58,000
or if your son

777
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:00,000
really wants to stay here because his friends are here,

778
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:02,000
then you're just going to live with it,

779
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:04,000
because this is not an experience for you,

780
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:06,000
this is an experience for your son.

781
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:08,000
and once again,

782
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:10,000
what is it all about?

783
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:12,000
what's this whole thing that we're involved in all about?

784
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:14,000
it's focused on the boy.

785
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:16,000
it's to give him certain experiences

786
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:18,000
and challenges and opportunities.

787
00:29:18,000 --> 00:29:20,000
he gets to do that with a group of his friends

788
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:22,000
and we get to have what Baden Powell

789
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:24,000
called the jollity of scouting.

790
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:26,000
it's a fun time-

791
00:29:26,000 --> 00:29:28,000
it should be a great time- where we grow

792
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:30,000
and we learn together.

793
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:32,000
when conflicts come up, we can certainly resolve them,

794
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:34,000
and when we're running the scouting program,

795
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:36,000
when we're doing what the program says,

796
00:29:36,000 --> 00:29:38,000
we not only avoid conflicts,

797
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:40,000
but when they inevitably arise-

798
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:42,000
and they will-

799
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:44,000
we have a way to resolve them.

800
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:46,000
so I hope that helps everybody.

801
00:29:46,000 --> 00:29:48,000
if you're in the middle of a similar situation

802
